--TEST--
Core\SignedCookie::open answers one RuntimeError for a tampered cookie, one that is not base64, one too short to be a cookie and one under a retired key, while a ring entry that was never a key is a LogicError instead — `rule:security/protocol-roster` and `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of four ways of not being an authentic cookie. Every PHP
// cookie signer this replaces distinguishes at least two of them — "bad
// signature" against "malformed" — and each distinction tells a forger which
// half of the attempt landed, and against a rotating ring, which key to aim
// at. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/signed_cookie.rs` is the home of that argument.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $retired = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];
string $cookie = Core\SignedCookie::seal("user=ada", $ring);

// Four more characters of the alphabet: still URL-safe base64 and still a
// legal length, so it decodes cleanly and reaches the tag check. This is the
// forgery an unauthenticated cookie hands back a plaintext for.
try {
    string $forged = Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie . "AAAA", $ring);
    echo "tamper accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $altered) {
    echo $altered->message, "\n";
}

// One character outside the alphabet, so it never decodes at all — and is
// refused with the same sentence rather than one about its spelling.
try {
    string $garbled = Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie . "?", $ring);
    echo "a non-base64 cookie accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notBase64) {
    echo $notBase64->message, "\n";
}

// The empty cookie: nothing to hold a nonce and a tag between them, which is
// what a browser sends when the cookie was cleared and the code path a naive
// signer treats as "no signature to check".
try {
    string $nothing = Core\SignedCookie::open("", $ring);
    echo "an empty cookie accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $tooShort) {
    echo $tooShort->message, "\n";
}

// An intact cookie under a ring that never held the key that sealed it — the
// state an operator reaches by rotating one key too many.
array<secret bytes> $strangers = [$retired];
try {
    string $wrong = Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, $strangers);
    echo "opened under a ring that never sealed it", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notThisRing) {
    echo $notThisRing->message, "\n";
}

// The two failures that are *not* about the cookie. A ring with nothing in it
// and a ring holding a `bytes` that was never a key are both program bugs, so
// they get the other class and a sentence that names the entry by its index.
array<secret bytes> $empty = [];
try {
    string $unsealable = Core\SignedCookie::seal("user=ada", $empty);
    echo "an empty ring sealed", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $noKeys) {
    echo $noKeys->message, "\n";
}

secret bytes $stunted = Core\Random::bytes(8);
array<secret bytes> $stuntedRing = [$stunted];
try {
    string $stillUnsealable = Core\SignedCookie::seal("user=ada", $stuntedRing);
    echo "sealed under an 8-octet key", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $notAKey) {
    echo $notAKey->message, "\n";
}

try {
    string $unopenable = Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, $stuntedRing);
    echo "opened under an 8-octet key", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $stillNotAKey) {
    echo $stillNotAKey->message, "\n";
}

// The other side of the bound: the cookie this class sealed still opens under
// the ring that sealed it, so the refusals above are about their arguments and
// not about `open` having become a member that refuses everything.
if (Core\SignedCookie::open($cookie, $ring) == "user=ada") {
    echo "and the cookie it sealed opens", "\n";
} else {
    echo "refused its own cookie", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\SignedCookie::open(): $cookie is not an authentic cookie under any key in $keys — it has been altered, it is not a cookie this application sealed, or the key it was sealed under has been retired
Core\SignedCookie::open(): $cookie is not an authentic cookie under any key in $keys — it has been altered, it is not a cookie this application sealed, or the key it was sealed under has been retired
Core\SignedCookie::open(): $cookie is not an authentic cookie under any key in $keys — it has been altered, it is not a cookie this application sealed, or the key it was sealed under has been retired
Core\SignedCookie::open(): $cookie is not an authentic cookie under any key in $keys — it has been altered, it is not a cookie this application sealed, or the key it was sealed under has been retired
Core\SignedCookie::seal(): $keys is empty, and a key ring needs at least the key that seals — Core\Crypto::generateKey() answers one.
Core\SignedCookie::seal(): $keys[0] is 8 octets, and a key is 32 — Core\Crypto::generateKey() answers one of the right length. The value is not quoted here, because a key does not belong in a log.
Core\SignedCookie::open(): $keys[0] is 8 octets, and a key is 32 — Core\Crypto::generateKey() answers one of the right length. The value is not quoted here, because a key does not belong in a log.
and the cookie it sealed opens
